/*--------------------------------*- C++ -*----------------------------------*\
  VMFLGPU007 -- CONSTANT PROPERTIES, exactly the four the manual prints on p.244:
      Density       = 1 kg/m3        -> equationOfState rhoConst
      Viscosity     = 0.0001 kg/m-s  -> transport const, mu
      Conductivity  = 1.408 W/m-K    -> carried as Pr, see below
      Specific heat = 10,000 J/kg-K  -> thermodynamics Cp
  "Fluid properties are considered constant" (manual p.244), so rhoConst + hConst
  + const transport reproduces that table EXACTLY and no property varies with T.

  WHY A COMPRESSIBLE-FORM SOLVER FOR AN INCOMPRESSIBLE CASE: rhoConst makes the
  density constant, so the flow is incompressible by construction. The reason for
  this route is the WALL TREATMENT -- the manual specifies standard wall
  functions, so the conductive sublayer is NOT resolved and the wall temperature
  (hence Nu, the gate quantity) is set by alphatJayatillekeWallFunction, which
  exists only on this path. A passive-scalar route would have had no thermal wall
  function and would have produced a wall temperature with no model behind it.

  OpenFOAM's "const" transport takes mu and Pr, not kappa. Pr is therefore
  DERIVED from the manual's own four numbers:
      Pr = mu * Cp / kappa = 1e-4 * 10000 / 1.408 = 0.7102272727272728
  and kappa is recovered EXACTLY: mu*Cp/Pr = 1.408 W/m-K. The comparator's
  selftest executes that round trip so the two cannot drift apart.

  molWeight is REQUIRED by the "specie" model but is INERT here: with rhoConst
  the density never consults it. 28.966 is the manual's own air value (p.236).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
FoamFile { version 2.0; format ascii; class dictionary; object thermophysicalProperties; }

thermoType
{
    type            heRhoThermo;
    mixture         pureMixture;
    transport       const;
    thermo          hConst;
    equationOfState rhoConst;
    specie          specie;
    energy          sensibleEnthalpy;
}

mixture
{
    specie          { molWeight  28.966; }
    equationOfState { rho        1.0; }
    thermodynamics  { Cp         10000; Hf 0; }
    transport       { mu         1e-4; Pr 0.7102272727272728; }
}

